    "lawCode": "Add §§230-e & 2805-z, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2113A\n\nSPONSOR: FELDER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nrequiring physicians and hospitals in a city with a population of one\nmillion or more to obtain the name of the school attended by school-aged\npatients and to include this information in their admission registration\nforms\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill provides that hospital, medical clinics, and private practice\nphysicians' offices in a city with a population of one million or more\ninclude a request for the name of the current school attendance of their\npatients.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would add a new section 230-e to the public health law, to\nrequire physicians in a city with a population of one million or more,\nto request and record the name and address of the school attended by\nschool-aged patients as part of the registration process and include\nthat information in the patients' medical registration form.\n\nThis bill would additionally add a new section 2805-2 to the public\nhealth law, to require hospitals and medical clinics in a city with a\npopulation of one million or more, to request, upon treatment of\npatients who are currently enrolled in school, the name of the school\nthat said patient attends and to include the same in the patient's\nrecords.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe death of 12 year-old Omar Rivera, who attended I.S. 211 in Brooklyn,\nfrom the methicillinresistant Staphylococcus aureus, commonly known as\nMRSA, and the increasing number of reported infected students across the\nstate, illustrates the need for health practitioners to be more respon-\nsive in alerting the affected community of their potential exposure to\ninfectious diseases, and of immediate preventative.\n\nThis bill seeks to do just that, in a responsive, responsible way.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect one year after the date upon which it shall\nhave become law.\n",
